<div style="text-align: center;">
<p><em><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YQZX0ams8dc">Let's watch this city burn</a></em><br />
<em><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YQZX0ams8dc">From the sky lies the top the world</a></em><br />
<em><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YQZX0ams8dc">Til there's nothing left of her</a></em><br />
<em><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YQZX0ams8dc">Let's watch this city burn the world</a></em></p>
</div>
<hr />
<p>This was a difficult article to write. It is my second most stressful article since my first, <a href="/scp-2238">SCP-2238</a>. It's about many things. An island, a culture, a political and cultural relationship, everything in between. After deleting the original version off the site, I wanted to quit. I felt like a failure. But, mlister forced me into rewriting it so my work wouldn't go to waste. I hated it at the time, but if it wasn't for them, I don't know if this article would have gone back up.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;What&nbsp;the&nbsp;hell&nbsp;is&nbsp;this&nbsp;thing?</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide&nbsp;whatever</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I first learned about filibusters while watching <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DqOC2f4zee4">this video</a> on the historical accuracy of the William Walker movie by CynicalHistorian, an excellent educational channel. The original idea was going to be a filibuster campaign on the city of Adytite itself, which I <em>believe</em> in canon is in Mongolia. However, realizing it would be too unrealistic for filibusters to attack Asia (generally they were active in Latin and South America), I think it would be more believable for it to take place in Polynesia.</p>
<p>I had several big fears writing this thing, most important my writing of (A) the native peoples and (B) the 'colonizing force' so to speak. I didn't want the native people to just be pawns in the colonizer's game. I wanted them to be active in their destiny, in some manner or form. They were capable of rebellion just like any group of people, capable of resisting just like any group of people. And the colonizer (Norman) was a big, personal fear. I didn't want him to be a stereotypical racist person who wants to take over the world with no regard for the lives of the natives (in the beginning, at least) &#8212; that's just not a compelling or interesting character, and not accurate to real life. But at the same time, I didn't want to show him in a sympathetic light.</p>
<p>So, with these two fears in mind, I wrote and rewrote the article. Early drafts failed at trying to delve into this theme (imperialism/colonialism), and later ones were too bloated, until I finally settled on this new version after the previous version (posted 4000 entry) failed.<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-433924-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-433924-1')">1</a></sup></p>
<p>I hope people enjoy it. I know it won't win, but, hey, who writes an article for a contest and expects it to win?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
Thank you to Kakroom, Avocadonut, Peppersghost, Othellocat, Cyantreuse, Darkstuff, and others for looking at this draft. They were a big help for making this whole thing possible, both in ideas, story progression, and general grammar. Another big thanks to Cyril, who made the sarkic.png picture above.
<p>Thank you to Absentmindednihilist and mlister for acting as emotional support throughout this nightmare of an article.</p>
